 Our route is one arrangement of attention. Other objects stand beside it; other histories could change its order. What matters is that following it gives us enough understanding to begin making such changes, together or alone.
 
+[Day Zero — Hello, world](day-zero.md) is the practical chapter of this preface. It prepares the working environment once. The making passages that follow assume we can already open, change and run the supplied project.
+
 We have to begin somewhere. The first room is already running.
 
 [^preface-room-history]: In 1968, Ivan E. Sutherland's experimental head-mounted display put its viewer inside a room drawn in wireframe. Turning the head changed the view; walls, ceiling and floor were already part of the experiment. See Ivan E. Sutherland, [“A Head-Mounted Three Dimensional Display”](https://doi.org/10.1145/1476589.1476686) (1968), especially “Results”; [accessible paper](https://archive.aec.at/media/assets/636b23b07bfd1b4f1d1154827ac9d9ab.pdf). The wireframe room and response to head movement are described there. The acknowledgments name a collective effort, and the funding note names ARPA, the Office of Naval Research and Bell Telephone Laboratories. This is one historical encounter with these problems, not a claim that the museum follows a single line of descent.
